<h2>The seven steps</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Find your event.</strong> Search by performer, team, city or category, or start from <a href="/buy-tickets-online">all events</a>, <a href="/concert-tickets-for-sale">concerts</a>, <a href="/game-day-tickets">sports</a> or <a href="/buy-broadway-tickets">Broadway and theater</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Check the date, time and venue.</strong> Confirm all three on the event page. Similar names and repeat dates are easy to mix up.</li>
  <li><strong>Choose how many tickets and which seats.</strong> Use the filters to narrow by price or section and compare a few options.</li>
  <li><strong>Review the full cost.</strong> Fees and taxes can be added at checkout. Review the full cost there before you pay.</li>
  <li><strong>Enter your details and pay.</strong> Use accurate contact information so your confirmation and tickets reach you.</li>
  <li><strong>Save your confirmation.</strong> An order confirmation is sent by email after payment. Keep your order ID.</li>
  <li><strong>Get your tickets.</strong> Follow the delivery instructions in your confirmation email.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Comparing seats before you pay</h3>
<p>Two seats with the same label can feel very different. Look at the section and row, note whether the event has assigned seating or general admission, and decide what matters most: a close view, an aisle or a lower price. Sports events usually have assigned seating, and theater shows often limit late entry, so read the event details. If cost is the deciding factor, see our <a href="/ticket-deals">ticket deals</a> and <a href="/tickets-promo-code">promo codes</a> pages, although availability changes.</p>

<h2>How to buy concert tickets</h2>
<p>To buy concert tickets, search for the artist, pick the city and date, then compare seats on the venue map. Many tours release tickets in stages: an artist or fan-club presale, a general sale, and then resale listings from sellers who bought earlier. Dates and rules for the first sale are set by the artist, promoter and venue, so check their official announcement for when concert tickets go on sale.</p>
<p>When a show sells out at the box office, tickets are often still listed by resale sellers, which is how people buy tickets to a sold-out concert. Prices are set by sellers and can be above or below face value, so compare a few sections first. Browse <a href="/concert-tickets-for-sale">concert tickets for sale</a>, or see <a href="/why-are-concert-tickets-so-expensive">why concert tickets cost what they do</a>.</p>

<h2>Where to buy tickets</h2>
<p>You can buy tickets from the venue box office, the official ticket seller for an event, or a resale marketplace. Seat Outlet is a resale marketplace: it lists seats from many sellers in one place and sends you to a guaranteed checkout. Use it to compare where to buy concert tickets, <a href="/game-day-tickets">sports tickets</a> and <a href="/buy-broadway-tickets">Broadway tickets</a> side by side. Wherever you buy, look for a clear guarantee, a secure checkout and a way to reach support.</p>

<h2>When to buy tickets</h2>
<p>There is no single best time. Popular shows can sell out early, while some events have tickets available until the day of the show. If you have a date and seats in mind, buying earlier gives you more choice. If you are flexible, compare listings over several days and use a price alert to hear when prices change. Our <a href="/ticket-deals">ticket deals</a> page has more ways to save.</p>

<h2>Delivery and entry</h2>
<p>Delivery depends on the event and the seller. It may be electronic, a mobile transfer or physical shipping, and some tickets are released closer to the event date, so tickets that have not arrived right away are not always a problem. If something looks wrong, <a href="/ticket-customer-service">contact us</a> well before doors open.</p>
<p>Most events accept mobile tickets, and printing is only needed if the event page says so. Bring your ticket and a government-issued ID if the venue requires one. The <a href="/ticket-faq">ticket FAQ</a> covers entry rules, age limits and what you can bring.</p>

<h2>Safe habits</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Only enter payment details on a page you reached from the site itself.</li>
  <li>Read the cancellation terms on the event page before you pay.</li>
  <li>Keep your confirmation email and a screenshot of your order.</li>
  <li>Be wary of anyone who asks you to pay outside the checkout.</li>
</ul>
<p>The Federal Trade Commission has more <a href="https://consumer.ftc.gov/articles/online-shopping" target="_blank" rel="noopener">tips for shopping online</a>. Our <a href="/blog/how-to-avoid-ticket-scams">guide to avoiding ticket scams</a> covers the ticket-specific ones.</p>

